    "questionText": "A 66-year-old man presents with a blood pressure of 240/132 mmHg, headache and blurred vision, and is admitted for a hypertensive emergency. He has stage 4 chronic kidney disease with a creatinine of 3.1 mg/dL. He is started on a continuous intravenous vasodilator at a high infusion rate. After 36 hours he becomes confused and tachypneic. His temperature is 37.1 C, blood pressure 118/70 mmHg, pulse 106/min and respirations 28/min. Arterial blood gas shows a high anion gap metabolic acidosis with a lactate of 7.2 mmol/L. Which mechanism best explains his deterioration?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Accumulation of an active nitric oxide donor causing excessive vasodilation and shock", "explanation": "Incorrect. Nitroprusside has a circulatory half-life of about 2 minutes and a hypotensive duration of only 1 to 10 minutes, so it cannot accumulate, and his blood pressure is now controlled rather than shocked. The lactate and altered mentation require a different explanation. This would be the correct answer if he were profoundly hypotensive immediately after the infusion rate was increased." },
      { "text": "Exhaustion of sulfur donor substrate for rhodanese, allowing cyanide accumulation", "explanation": "Correct. Red blood cells take up nitroprusside and release nitric oxide plus cyanide, and cyanide is detoxified by mitochondrial rhodanese, which transfers sulfur from a donor to form thiocyanate. That reaction is saturable, so a large or prolonged exposure exhausts the sulfur donor supply and free cyanide accumulates, blocking oxidative phosphorylation and producing lactic acidosis with altered mental status. His high infusion rate over 36 hours is the classic setting, and his renal failure compounds the problem because thiocyanate is eliminated renally with a half-life of about 3 days that may lengthen two- to threefold." },
      { "text": "Hepatic acetylation deficiency producing antinuclear antibodies and a lupus-like state", "explanation": "Incorrect. Slow NAT2 acetylation causing a lupus-like syndrome is a hydralazine problem, and it presents subacutely with rash, arthralgia, myalgia and fever rather than acute confusion and lactic acidosis over 36 hours. This would be the correct answer if he had been on high-dose oral hydralazine for months and developed a malar rash with joint pain." },
      { "text": "Dopamine D1 receptor overstimulation producing natriuresis and prerenal azotemia", "explanation": "Incorrect. D1 agonism describes fenoldopam, which is actually favored in renal compromise because it does not reduce renal blood flow, and it does not produce lactic acidosis or encephalopathy. This would be the correct answer if he had received fenoldopam and developed flushing, headache and a rise in intraocular pressure." },
      { "text": "Baroreceptor-mediated reflex tachycardia producing demand ischemia and lactate generation", "explanation": "Incorrect. A pulse of 106/min in a patient whose pressure has just been lowered is expected and is far too modest to generate a lactate of 7.2 mmol/L with encephalopathy in a hemodynamically stable patient. Reflex tachycardia is managed with a beta blocker, not by treating a metabolic poison. This would be the correct answer if he had known coronary disease and presented with anginal chest pain and ischemic ECG changes." }
    ],
