      { "text": "Administer intravenous N-acetylcysteine to replenish hepatic glutathione stores fully", "explanation": "Incorrect. Glutathione repletion is the antidote strategy for acetaminophen toxicity, which presents with transaminase elevation rather than an elevated venous oxygen saturation and lactic acidosis on a vasodilator infusion. This would be the correct answer if she had an acetaminophen overdose with rising aminotransferases." },
      { "text": "Administer hydroxocobalamin, which binds cyanide as cyanocobalamin", "explanation": "Correct. This is cyanide accumulation from prolonged nitroprusside infusion, in which cells cannot extract oxygen, producing lactic acidosis with a paradoxically high venous oxygen saturation. Hydroxocobalamin combines directly with cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, a nontoxic form of vitamin B12 that is then excreted. The alternative antidote strategy taught in this lecture is sodium nitrite followed by sodium thiosulfate, which supplies the sulfur donor that rhodanese needs to convert cyanide to thiocyanate. Stopping the infusion is necessary but not sufficient once cyanide has accumulated." }
