      { "text": "Nitroprusside by infusion, because its effect can be titrated minute to minute", "explanation": "Incorrect. Nitroprusside is an intravenous agent for hypertensive emergency, not chronic outpatient therapy, and prolonged infusion risks cyanide accumulation. She needs an oral drug for months. This would be the correct answer if she presented with an acute hypertensive crisis requiring minute-to-minute titration in an intensive care setting." },
      { "text": "Phenoxybenzamine, because irreversible blockade gives stable control between doses", "explanation": "Incorrect. Phenoxybenzamine is a nonselective alpha blocker used for pheochromocytoma, not for essential hypertension, and it causes orthostatic hypotension and tachycardia. This would be the correct answer if she had a catecholamine-secreting adrenal tumor identified during pregnancy." },
      { "text": "Methyldopa, because it does not reduce placental or fetal renal blood flow", "explanation": "Correct. Methyldopa is the alpha-2 agonist retained for one main modern indication, the management of hypertension in pregnancy. It is not teratogenic and does not reduce blood flow to the placenta, to the mother's kidneys or to the fetal kidneys, which is why it remains one of the important agents for chronic use in this setting. It lowers blood pressure after conversion in sympathetic neurons to alpha-methylnorepinephrine, a false transmitter acting at central alpha-2 receptors, and its side effects include sedation, bradycardia and constipation. For hypertensive emergency during pregnancy or postpartum, hydralazine is the agent named in this lecture." }
